// Unit 3 Lesson 1 Ã¢â‚¬â€ Nutrition Fundamentals (30 questions)


::U3L1-01::Which nutrient category is an important energy source? {=Carbohydrate ~Protein ~Vitamin ~Water}

::U3L1-02::Which nutrient supports growth, maintenance, and tissue repair? {=Protein ~Water ~Mineral ~Fiber}

::U3L1-03::Which statement best describes dietary fat? {=It supports energy, cells, and absorption of certain vitamins. ~It should always be avoided. ~It is a micronutrient. ~It automatically causes poor health.}

::U3L1-04::Which two groups are micronutrients? {=Vitamins and minerals ~Carbohydrates and proteins ~Fats and water ~Fiber and calories}

::U3L1-05::Why is water essential even though it provides no calories? {=It supports fluid balance, transport, and temperature regulation. ~It replaces every nutrient. ~It builds muscle without training. ~It makes hydration needs identical.}

::U3L1-06::Fiber is best described as {=a carbohydrate component in plant foods that supports digestive and other health functions. ~a vitamin that supplies calories. ~a mineral found only in sports drinks. ~a protein supplement.}

::U3L1-07::A calorie is {=a unit used to measure energy. ~a nutrient that should be avoided. ~a type of vitamin. ~a measure of body size.}

::U3L1-08::An electrolyte is {=a mineral involved in fluid balance and other body functions. ~a carbohydrate required after every activity. ~a measure of food quality. ~a protein that replaces water.}

::U3L1-09::Which statement about hydration is most accurate? {=Needs vary with activity, environment, duration, individual needs, and intake. ~Everyone needs exactly the same amount. ~Only athletes need water. ~A sports drink is required for every activity.}

::U3L1-10::Maya practices outdoors for 90 minutes in warm weather after drinking little. Which evidence most strongly supports paying attention to hydration? {=Duration, warm conditions, activity, and limited fluid occur together. ~Maya is a student. ~The activity is scheduled after school. ~No body weight is provided.}

::U3L1-11::Why should one symptom not be used alone to diagnose dehydration? {=A symptom can have multiple causes and requires context. ~Symptoms never matter. ~Hydration is unrelated to health. ~Only body size matters.}

::U3L1-12::Which conclusion about sports drinks is best supported? {=They are not automatically required for every activity; the context matters. ~They are necessary after every walk. ~They replace all food. ~They make hydration needs identical.}

::U3L1-13::Which set contains only macronutrients? {=Carbohydrate, protein, and fat ~Vitamins, minerals, and water ~Fiber, vitamins, and minerals ~Water, calcium, and iron}

::U3L1-14::Which statement correctly compares macronutrients and micronutrients? {=Macronutrients are needed in relatively larger amounts; vitamins and minerals are micronutrients. ~Only micronutrients support health. ~Water is a macronutrient because it is essential. ~Macronutrients are always unhealthy.}

::U3L1-15::A meal includes rice, beans, vegetables, avocado, fruit, and water. What is the strongest conclusion? {=Several nutrient categories and hydration are represented, and foods may contain multiple nutrients. ~The meal is perfect for every person. ~Only carbohydrate is represented. ~Its quality can be proven by appearance.}

::U3L1-16::A student says, Ã¢â‚¬Å“This food contains fat, so it is unhealthy.Ã¢â‚¬Â What is the best correction? {=Fat has necessary functions; evaluate type, amount, overall pattern, and context. ~All fat should be replaced with protein. ~One food determines the whole dietary pattern. ~Body weight proves whether the claim is correct.}

::U3L1-17::A student says, Ã¢â‚¬Å“Extra protein automatically creates muscle.Ã¢â‚¬Â Which response is most accurate? {=Muscle adaptation also depends on training, recovery, overall nutrition, and individual factors. ~Protein never has a role in repair. ~Supplements are required. ~Carbohydrates prevent muscle adaptation.}

::U3L1-18::Which lens asks whether a dietary pattern includes different foods and nutrient sources? {=Variety ~Appearance ~Popularity ~Restriction}

::U3L1-19::Which lens considers whether needs are sufficiently supported? {=Adequacy ~Advertising ~Endorsement ~Body size}

::U3L1-20::Which factor belongs in the context of a dietary pattern? {=Culture, access, cost, preference, and schedule ~Follower count only ~Appearance only ~One isolated ingredient only}

::U3L1-21::Why is an overall dietary pattern more useful than labeling one food Ã¢â‚¬Å“goodÃ¢â‚¬Â or Ã¢â‚¬Å“badÃ¢â‚¬Â? {=It considers variety, balance, adequacy, and context over time. ~It guarantees one perfect diet. ~It ignores access and culture. ~It measures health by body size.}

::U3L1-22::Which statement best reflects nutrient density? {=It considers useful nutrients relative to a food's overall energy and content context. ~It labels people by what they eat. ~It proves one food is always healthy. ~It means the food has no calories.}

::U3L1-23::Which is the strongest evidence-based reasoning chain? {=Scenario fact Ã¢â€ â€™ relevant nutrition concept Ã¢â€ â€™ limited supported conclusion ~Opinion Ã¢â€ â€™ appearance Ã¢â€ â€™ universal rule ~Popularity Ã¢â€ â€™ product purchase Ã¢â€ â€™ health claim ~Body size Ã¢â€ â€™ food label Ã¢â€ â€™ diagnosis}

::U3L1-24::Riley cycles indoors for 25 minutes and has water. A post says a sports drink is always required. What is the best evaluation? {=The claim is too absolute; evaluate duration, intensity, environment, and individual need. ~The post is correct because it is online. ~Riley should avoid water. ~The claim is proven by product popularity.}

::U3L1-25::Noor volunteers outdoors in warm weather and has limited transportation and food choices. Which analysis is most appropriate? {=Consider hydration, access, cost, available foods, and realistic alternatives. ~Recommend an expensive product without considering access. ~Judge the pattern by body size. ~Require a medical diet.}

::U3L1-26::Which statement respects cultural and practical variation? {=Health-supporting dietary patterns can exist within different cultures, budgets, and preferences. ~One cuisine is universally superior. ~Cost never affects food choices. ~Personal preference proves nutrition quality.}

::U3L1-27::Which conclusion is NOT supported by a personÃ¢â‚¬â„¢s appearance? {=The quality of the personÃ¢â‚¬â„¢s nutrition and hydration pattern ~The color of the person's shirt ~Whether the person is indoors ~Whether a photo was posted}

::U3L1-28::Which recommendation avoids food moralization? {=Evaluate how the choice fits the overall pattern and context. ~Call every carbohydrate bad. ~Call one meal perfect. ~Judge the person for eating fat.}

::U3L1-29::Which source behavior raises a credibility concern? {=An account sells a product while offering only testimonials and absolute claims. ~A source explains evidence and limitations. ~A source identifies relevant qualifications. ~A source distinguishes estimates from exact values.}

::U3L1-30::Which response best connects nutrition to physical activity? {=Nutrients and hydration may influence available energy, recovery, and performance, but needs vary. ~One food guarantees performance. ~Supplements are necessary for all students. ~Appearance proves fitness and nutrition quality.}

